"""
hedging_effectiveness.py
========================
Hedging effectiveness of temperature index-based insurance for French winter wheat.

Methodology follows Pelka, Musshoff & Finger (2014) — "Hedging effectiveness of
weather index-based insurance in China" — adapted for:
  - Temperature indices (HDD / CDD at T_REF=18°C) instead of precipitation
  - French winter wheat (soft wheat, blé tendre d'hiver) instead of Chinese maize
  - NN-OU (CalibNet) Monte Carlo simulation for fair premium instead of burn analysis

Methodological steps (mirroring paper exactly):
  1. Load regional wheat yield CSV; compute surface-area-weighted yield per region-year
  2. Remove deterministic trend via MM-estimator robust regression (Finger 2010);
     linear vs quadratic model selected per region via Wald test (paper Section 3.1)
  3. Convert de-trended yield to revenue at fixed wheat price (analogous to paper eq. 2)
  4. For each region x index type (HDD, CDD): build monthly index matrix for all years
  5. Select best accumulation month by highest significant |Pearson r| with de-trended
     yield (paper Section 3.1):
     - Positive r  -> put option:  payoff = max(Strike - I, 0)
     - Negative r  -> call option: payoff = max(I - Strike, 0)
     - Month must pass p < 0.10 significance threshold; skipped otherwise
  6. Strike = long-run average of realised index (paper Section 3.1)
  7. Fair premium = E[payoff] estimated via NN-OU Monte Carlo, averaged across years
  8. Optimal hedge ratio z* via Johnson (1960) / Miranda (1991):
       a) z*_SD  = argmin Var(hedged revenue)  -> closed-form OLS formula
       b) z*_VaR = argmin VaR90%(hedged revenue) -> grid search
     Solved SEPARATELY; each used independently for its own HE metric (paper Table II)
  9. Hedging effectiveness = relative reduction of SD (under z*_SD) and
     VaR90% (under z*_VaR) — paper eq. 3
 10. Output table equivalent to paper Table II
"""

